"need": "The field faces significant challenges in reconciling atomistic mechanisms with macroscopic mechanical behavior, particularly in nanocrystalline and superplastic materials. A primary unmet need is the accurate determination of stacking fault energies, as current experimental methods based on dislocation separation require re-evaluation due to the influence of maximum stacking energy. Furthermore, the interpretation of fundamental phenomena, such as the yield point in germanium, remains unresolved, with no new insights emerging for over a decade. Methodologically, there is a critical gap in linking microstructural evolution, such as dislocation structure changes during mechanical alloying, to measurable properties like yield stress and grain boundary mobility. Existing constitutive models struggle to consistently describe the transition from grain-size-dependent strength to grain-boundary-dominated flow in the nanocrystalline regime. Additionally, the role of impurity segregation and grain boundary character distribution in controlling dynamic embrittlement and superplastic deformation requires more precise quantitative frameworks. Finally, integrating temperature effects and high strain-rate dynamics into discrete dislocation models remains an open problem, limiting the predictive capability of simulations for complex thermoplastic processes."
